skills/repo-forensics/tests/*
skills/forensify/tests/*

# Integrity checksums (SHA256 hashes trigger entropy scanner)
skills/repo-forensics/checksums.json

# Threat pattern references (documentation describes the attacks we detect)
skills/repo-forensics/references/*.md

# Scanner scripts and core (contain regex patterns for the attacks they detect)
skills/repo-forensics/scripts/scan_*.py
skills/repo-forensics/scripts/parse_pnpm_lock.py
skills/repo-forensics/scripts/aggregate_json.py
skills/repo-forensics/scripts/forensics_core.py
skills/repo-forensics/scripts/ioc_manager.py

# IOC inventories and rulepacks (carry attack literals as DATA, same reason as
# the scanner scripts above). Without these, scanning this repo with its own
# scanners reports the IOC list itself as an infection. The data/ globs are
# covered by checksums.json / *.sig, so tampering there is caught by integrity
# verification. Added 2026-08-06 with the Shai-Hulud family behavioral-coverage
# expansion, which widened the self-match surface.
skills/repo-forensics/data/*.json
skills/repo-forensics/data/rulepacks/*.json
# iocs/ is NOT in checksums.json, so a wildcard here would let a dropped payload
# (e.g. iocs/actionsSecrets.json) be silently ignored by BOTH content scanning
# and integrity. List ONLY the two known-legit inventories by exact name so any
# other JSON appearing under iocs/ is still scanned as content.
iocs/latest.json
iocs/rulepacks.json

# SKILL.md (documents the attack types we catch)
skills/repo-forensics/SKILL.md

# README describes the attack types we detect — without this, the newly
# wired correlation engine flags meta-documentation as install-time exfil.
# Added 2026-04-05 with the aggregate_json.py correlation wiring.
README.md
CHANGELOG.md

